<article class="env-lemma" id="08HZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08HZ">Lemma <span data-tag="08HZ">20.33.5</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of ringed spaces. Suppose that $X = U \cup V$ is a union of two open subsets. Denote $a = f|_ U : U \to Y$, $b = f|_ V : V \to Y$, and $c = f|_{U \cap V} : U \cap V \to Y$. For every object $E$ of $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$ there exists a distinguished triangle </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  Rf_*E \to Ra_*(E|_ U) \oplus Rb_*(E|_ V) \to Rc_*(E|_{U \cap V}) \to Rf_*E[1]  \]
</div>
  <p> This triangle is functorial in $E$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a K-injective complex $\mathcal{I}^\bullet $ representing $E$. We may assume $\mathcal{I}^ n$ is an injective object of $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O}_ X)$ for all $n$, see Injectives, Theorem <a href="/tag/079P" data-tag="079P">19.12.6</a>. Then $Rf_*E$ is computed by $f_*\mathcal{I}^\bullet $. Similarly for $U$, $V$, and $U \cap V$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/08BS" data-tag="08BS">20.32.1</a>. Hence the distinguished triangle of the lemma is the distinguished triangle associated (by Derived Categories, Section <a href="/tag/014Z" data-tag="014Z">13.12</a> and especially Lemma <a href="/tag/0152" data-tag="0152">13.12.1</a>) to the short exact sequence of complexes 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to f_*\mathcal{I}^\bullet \to a_*\mathcal{I}^\bullet |_ U \oplus b_*\mathcal{I}^\bullet |_ V \to c_*\mathcal{I}^\bullet |_{U \cap V} \to 0.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       This is a short exact sequence of complexes by Lemma <a href="/tag/01EC" data-tag="01EC">20.8.3</a> and the fact that $R^1f_*\mathcal{I} = 0$ for an injective object $\mathcal{I}$ of $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. The final statement follows from the functoriality of the construction in Injectives, Theorem <a href="/tag/079P" data-tag="079P">19.12.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>